## 1. Introduction
Recently, potassium-ion batteries (PIBs) have attracted the intense attention of some researchers due to the abundant reserves, low price of element potassium and the low standard electric potential $\mathrm{(E^{\circ}\ (L i^{+}/L i)}=-3.04\ \mathrm{V},\mathrm{E^{\circ}\ (N a^{+}/N a)}=-2.71\ \mathrm{V},\mathrm{E^{\circ}}$ $(\mathrm{K^{+}}/\mathrm{K})=-2.93\mathrm{V},$ , all vs standard hydrogen redox potential).[1-3] Based on development requirement of PIBs, many cathode materials for PIBs have been investigated such as $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{CoO}_{2}[4{-}6]$ , $\mathrm{K_{x}M n O_{2}[7]}$ , $\mathrm{KTi}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}[3]$ , organic molecules[8], metal-organic frameworks[9-11] etc. However, the capacity of these materials can barely exceed $200~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . There is no doubt that low capacity limits the further development of PIBs and cannot meet human requirement of production and living. Meanwhile, too large ionic radius of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ $^+\ (0.138\ \mathrm{\nm})$ is also a great challenge to overcome through the efforts of many researchers as early as possible. So, it is imperative to explore and research new high-capacity and great performance cathode materials for PIBs.
As is known to all, S-based and Se-based cathode materials vastly enhance electrochemistry performance of lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) and sodium-ion batteries (SIBs). When selenium and sulfur are compared from the inherent nature of each other, selenium not only has as high volumetric capacity density $(3253~\mathrm{mAh~cm^{-3}})$ as sulfur $(3467~\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{~h~cm}^{-3})$ ) but also has better electrical conductivity $(1\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~m}^{-1})$ than sulfur $(0.5{\times}10^{-27}\mathrm{~S~m~}^{-1})$ . Besides, selenium can be stabilized under the action of special restriction easily.[12] Chen’s group and Fu’s group reported respectively
PANI@CMK-3/sulfur composite and SPAN composite as the cathode for rechargeable K-S batteries with initial specific capacities of $512.7~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and 710 $\mathrm{\mA\h\g^{-1}}$ ，which maintained $202.3\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 50 cycles at a current density of 50 $\mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $387~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles at 0.5C, separately.[13,14] But it was not still high based on the theoretical capacity of element sulfur (1675 mA h g Lately, Guo’s group reported selenium/carbonized-PAN composite (c-PAN-Se) to work as the cathode material for new K-Se battery for the first time.[15] The Se-based cathode contributed an initial specific capacity of $652\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{~h~g~}^{-1}\mathrm{\at\}0.2\mathrm{\C}$ and maintained a reversible capacity of $396~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles based on the theoretical capacity of element selenium $(675\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}},$ ). More than that, the formed composite effectively inhibited the formation of polyselenides and avoided their dissolution during discharge-charge process. Thus, the Se-based cathode material delivered a good electrochemistry performance for PIBs.
On account of the unique geometrical structure, large specific surface areas and abundant internal pores, the metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are widely applied to numerous fields such as fuel storage[16], gas adsorption[17,18], electrocatalysis and photocatalysis[19,20]， medical drugs[21,22] etc. In addition, the derivatives of metal-organic frameworks can usually inherit excellent characteristic natures of MOFs. Therefore, many MOFs-derived porous matrix materials were used to confine S or Se to apply to Li-S batteries[23,24], Li-Se batteries[25,26], Na-S batteries at room temperature[27-29] and Na-Se batteries[30,31]. Unfortunately, however, there are not any metal-organic frameworks derived porous matrix materials loading Se to be applied to rapid-developed PIBs up to now.
In this work, the N-doped porous carbon matrix derived from the precursor ZIF-67 with a size of about \~ $\cdot250\:\mathrm{nm}$ was synthesized and confined element selenium to use firstly as the cathode of rechargeable K-Se batteries. After formation of the metal-organic frameworks-derived porous carbon (MDPC), it provides enough storage space to host a large account of selenium. Simultaneously, the in-situ N -doping helps to improve the electronic conductivity of MDPC. As a result, the Se/MDPC cathode exhibits high capacity and good rate capability in rechargeable K-Se batteries.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1 Preparation of MDPC
All the chemical reagents used in the preparation process were analytical grade, purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Industrial Co. (China). In a typical process, 1454 mg of $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}\bullet6H_{2}O}$ was dissolved into $100~\mathrm{ml}$ of methanol and stirred for $30\mathrm{min}$ to form a clear red solution. Then, $4100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of 2-methylimidazole $\mathrm{(C_{4}H_{6}N_{2})}$ ) was dissolved into $100~\mathrm{ml}$ of methanol and stirred for $30~\mathrm{min}$ to form a clear transparent solution. Afterwards, this solution was poured into the above-mentioned red solution and the mixed solution was kept for about 24 hours at room temperature. Finally, the purple precursor ZIF-67 was collected by filtration, washed with ethanol for three times and dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the oven for $8\mathrm{~h~}$ . The dried precursor was heated at $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under Ar protection for $3\mathrm{~h~}$ with a heating speed of $5~{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , after which the black product was transferred to the 2M ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ solution to remove the Co species and labeled as MDPC.
## 2.2 Preparation of Se/MDPC composite
The MDPC and selenium powder mixture with weight ratio of 1:2 was heated at $260~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at Ar protection for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ with a heating speed of $5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ min-1 to produce Se/MDPC composite. For comparing, the Se/AB mixture was prepared by the same approach.
## 2.3 Materials characterization
The crystal structure of all the samples was examined via X-ray diffractometer with Cu K-alpha radiation $\lambda{=}1.5406\mathrm{nm}$ ). Raman patterns of all the samples were obtained by taking advantage of InviaRefl (Renishaw, UK) with $532.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser light from 2000 to $100~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . The BET specific surface areas and pore size distributions of MDPC and Se/MDPC composite were analyzed by Quadrasorbevo 2QDS-MP-30 (Quantachrome Instruments, USA). The Se content of Se/MDPC composite was detected by thermogravimetric analyzer (TGA, Q50). XPS (X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy) characterization of Se/MDPC composite was conducted on ESCALAB 250Xi electron spectrometer. The morphologies of ZIF-67, MDPC and Se/MDPC composite were observed using field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, JSM-7800F) .The transition electron microscope (TEM, JEM-2100) was used to observe the microstructures of MDPC and Se/MDPC composite.
## 2.4 Electrochemical measurements
CR2032 coin cells assembled in Ar-filled glove box were used to test electrochemical performance of the cathode material. Se/MDPC composite, acetylene black (AB) and PVDF with weight ratio of 7:2:1 were mixed in NMP dispersing agent and gently grinded to form homogeneous slurry. Afterwards，the slurry was covered on copper foil by blade coating and dried for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the oven. About 1.0 $1.8~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ active material (about $0.37\sim0.67\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\mathrm{Se})$ was coated on the cathode electrode. The Se/AB mixture was made by the same approach. In every coin cell, Se/MDPC composite, glass fiber, potassium foil respectively worked as cathode, separator and anode. Solid $\mathrm{KPF}_{6}$ was dissolved in EC and PC component solvent (v : $\mathbf{v}=1:1\mathbf{\Omega}_{\cdot}\mathbf{\Omega}$ ) to form 1M solution as electrolyte of K-Se batteries. $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the electrolyte is added into every cell to infiltrate the glass fiber adequately. Similarly, the Se/AB coin cells were assembled for comparison. Arbin Instruments testing System was used to conduct the Cyclic voltammetry (CV) at $0.1~\mathrm{mV~s}^{-1}$ between 2.3 and 0.7 V. And LAND instrument testing system was to carry out the galvanostatic discharge-charge.
## 3. Result and discussion
The whole synthetic process of the Se/MDPC composite is clearly demonstrated in Scheme 1. The mixed solution is kept standing for 24h to produce the purple precursor by precipitation method. Then, the precursor is converted to the porous carbon with abundant pores through a carbonization and acidification procedure and the MDPC matrix shows a homogeneous and hollow polyhedral structure. Eventually, the element selenium was effectively encapsulated into pores of the porous carbon matrix by the melt-infiltrating method.
As is observed in Figure 1a, the diffraction peaks of MDPC root respectively in the graphitized carbon (about 26 °) [25] and residue Co (about 45 °, 76.5 °) conforming to PDF 15-0806. Meanwhile, for Se/MDPC composite, except for the diffraction peaks of carbon and Co, the diffraction peaks of Se can be clearly observed, indicating that selenium has successfully entered into the pores of porous carbon matrix. For better comparison and analysis, the diffraction peaks of the precursor ZIF-67 are displayed in Figure S1, which is very consistent with previous reports.[32]
As can be further seen from Raman spectroscopy in Figure 1b, the D and G band of MDPC and Se/MDPC composite both appear on $1320~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $1580~\mathrm{{\cm}^{-1}}$ , corresponding to the amorphous structure and graphitized structure respectively. And the intensity of G band is lower than that of D band, indicating that MDPC has partly been graphitized.[26] As for pristine selenium, three peaks at near 144, 460 and 235 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ indicate that it includes both ring-structure $\left(\mathrm{Se}_{8}\right)$ and chain-structure.[30,33] However, only one peak located at $260~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ can be seen in Se/MDPC composite, indicating that it is a ring-structure ${\mathrm{Se}}_{8}$ with a suitable size, which can easily enter into the pores of MDPC.
Figure 2a shows the BET specific surface areas of MDPC and Se/MDPC composite. It can be seen that the BET specific surface areas of MDPC is very high up to $511~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , which is similar to previous report [31], while the BET specific surface areas of Se/MDPC composite is as low as $13~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The sharp decrease of specific surface area fully illustrates that selenium is encapsulated into porous carbon matrix by melt-infiltrating method. Figure 2b shows the pore size distributions of MDPC and Se/MDPC composite, which indicates that MDPC possess ample micropores and mesopores. And the existence of mesopores [34,35] from porous carbon matrix is further proved by the hysteresis loop at ${\bf N}_{2}$ pressures $(\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}=0.45{\sim}0.95$ ). After taking in selenium, a mass of mesopores disappear, indicating that ring-structure selenium is confined in the pores of MDPC. As is displayed in Figure 2c, the selenium content of Se/MDPC composite is high up to $53\%$ measured via TGA at ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere. It can be seen from the TG curve that there still exists a weight loss at ${\sim}550)\mathrm{\Omega}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which is due to the existence of $\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}$ (Figure S2). And as is shown in Figure S3, the energy difference of $15.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ between the two peaks respectivlely at 780.7 and $796.6~\mathrm{eV}$ implies the Co species exist as ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ion.[31]
The result of XPS analysis in Figure 2 d, e and f makes clear that elements and chemical state of the Se/MDPC composite. The existence of C, N and Se element can be obviously observed from the composite. In Figure 2d, the peaks of C1s spectra located at 284.5, 285.6, 286.7 and 288.1 eV respectively conform to C-C, $\mathrm{C=N}$ , C-N and C=O.[36-38] The spectra in Figure 2e shows the different states of nitrogen element, including pyrrolic N (399.3 eV), graphitic N $(400.9{\mathrm{~eV}})$ and oxidized N (402.6 eV). The existence of N element signifies that the polyhedral porous carbon matrix is N-doped in situ. In addition, the electrical conductivity of carbon can be greatly improved through a large number of external defects and active sites.[39] For spectra of Se 3d, peaks located at about 55.1 and $55.9\mathrm{eV}$ in Figure 2f are conformed to Se $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and Se $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ respectively, while the peak at about $58.8\mathrm{eV}$ is corresponding to Se-O.
