Figure 4a shows discharge-charge voltage profiles of Se/MDPC at different rate between $0.7~\mathrm{V}$ and $2.3{\mathrm{~V}}.$ The voltages of the reduction peaks are corresponding with the double discharge plateaus. With the current density increasing gradually, both the discharge plateau and discharge capacity decrease. And this case is possibly caused by high ohmic and kinetic overpotentials.[23] The CV curves are tested at 0.1 $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{v}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ between $2.3\mathrm{~V~}$ and $0.7\mathrm{V},$ , as is displayed in Figure S6. Figure 4b presents the galvanostatic discharge-charge curves at $0.2~\mathrm{C}$ and their plateau value is in good agreement with the data of CV curves. The irreversible capacity of the first discharge-charge process may cause from the formation of solid electrolyte interphase film.
As shown in Figure 4c, the cathode material of half cells delivers good rate capacity, discharge specific capacity of which are respectively 405, 267, 211, 150 and $101~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 0.05 C, 0.1 C, 0.2 C, 0.5 C, 1 C, respectively. The rate capacity of the first cycle at $0.05\mathrm{C}$ activation is presented in the Figure S7. When the current density returns $0.05~\mathrm{C}.$ , the specific capacity still maintains around 297 mA h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, the coulombic efficiency of the rate testing almost keeps over nearly ${\sim}90~\%$ , as is displayed in Figure S8. Moreover, the Se/MDPC composite delivers good cycling performance in Figure 4d, which still remains discharge capacity of about 130 mA h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with coulombic efficiency over $95\%$ until 100 cycles. The capacity attenuation might result from the large $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ radius that causes the structure partial collapse of cathode material. As a contrast, the cycling performance the Se/AB mixture is displayed in Figure S9 and the capacity that contributed is much lower than that of the Se/MDPC composite. And the existence of $\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}$ cannot impact on the capacity because its contribution is too low (Figure S10). Besides, different Se content of Se/MDCP composite ( $55\ \%$ and $60\%$ ) is prepared to test the cycling performance at $0.2~\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S11) and the capacity they can contribute is lower than that of composite with $53\%$ of Se content, which may be due to bulk Se adhered to surfaces of carbon matrix.[31]
The mechanism schematic diagram is clearly shown in Fig. 4e. And the shuttle effect can effectively be overcome by the space limitation, as is proved in Figure S12. The obviously darker color on glass fiber separator using Se/AB as the cathode can be observed. Moreover, the Se/MDPC composite still maintains the polyhedral shape after cycling 100 cycles at 0.2 C (Figure S13) , which demonstrates the ability of composite to buffer the volume change effectively.
The reason of all good performance of the as-obtained composite can be ascribed to the porous structure and good electronic conductivity of N-doping carbon matrix. A large amount of pores with hierarchical size from MDPC can provide enough storage space for selenium and alleviate the volume expansion problem. The in-situ N doping is conductive to enhancing electronic conductivity of carbon. The formed composite material is helpful to fill up the deficiencies of selenium and gives play to its advantages of selenium.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, MOFs derived N-doped porous carbon matrix has been successfully synthesized and the Se/MDPC composite was applied as the cathode for rechargeable K-Se batteries for the first time. The hollow structure provides enough storage space to accommodate selenium and the good electronic conductivity of carbon matrix guarantees the good electrochemistry performance. The electrochemical results show that the Se-based cathode material contributes good cycling performance and rate performance for rechargeable K-Se batteries. We believe that the development of K-Se batteries is conducive to promoting the progress of energy storage systems, solving the energy crisis as well as providing a new research idea for developing the next generation of clean energy system.
